    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:fb5db666cae6efb7",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "6fd591f6aaeaf6d59c09787b60ea877be99d70d222bab7463d640c116d8ff3af",
      "text": "Full-day excursion, entrance fee and lunch included\n\nSoaring almost vertically more than 2,000 meters above the Patagonian steppe, the granite towers of Torres del Paine dominate the landscape — but the park holds much more: azure lakes, trails through emerald forests, rushing rivers and one great, gleaming glacier. In the morning — a stop at Milodon Cave on Cerro Benítez hill, where in 1895 the remains of a prehistoric ground sloth that lived here 8,000–10,000 years ago were discovered. Next — panoramas of Sofía Lagoon, the Prat mountain range, Lake Porteño and Lake Toro; the Mirador Grey viewpoint overlooking the lake, where icebergs calved from the glacier drift on the water.\n\nA walk through lenga forest to the lakeshore, lunch at a local restaurant, viewpoints over Lake Pehoé and the 18-meter Salto Grande waterfall, as well as Lake Nordenskjöld and Laguna Amarga.\n\nOptional: a private helicopter excursion over the park's glaciers and peaks ($800–1,200/hour), available as an add-on to the day's program.\n\nHotel: The Singular Patagonia, Singular Room.",
